---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base title="About" description="Why Backstory exists, how to read it, how each issue is made, and who runs it.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>About Backstory</h1>
      <p>There is always more to the story.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="text-page">
      <p class="lead">
        Every day brings headlines about elections, economic trouble, wars and social change. The news keeps us informed
        about what is happening. Understanding why it is happening usually means looking past the headline.
      </p>
      <p>
        Behind every big story sits a tangle of history, economic incentives, political decisions and competing interests.
        You need that background to form a view of your own, and finding it usually takes hours of reading. Backstory
        exists to make it quicker.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each day, Backstory takes one story in the news and explains the context behind it: how we got here, who wants
        what, why the problem is so hard to solve, and what might happen next.
        Topics alternate between <strong>World</strong> stories and <strong>Singapore</strong> stories,
        and every issue is written for any reader, wherever they live.
      </p>
      <p>
        The aim is not to tell you what to think. It is to give you enough context to think it through yourself, in under
        thirty minutes a day.
      </p>

      <h2>How to read it</h2>
      <p>
        Think of Backstory as a daily habit for understanding the world. Each issue is built around the questions that
        matter most: Why is this happening? How did we get here? What makes it so hard to fix? What happens next?
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Read at your own pace.</strong> Each issue takes about 15 to 30 minutes, short enough to fit into a
          commute or a lunch break.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Skip to what interests you.</strong> Short on time? Every issue has a numbered contents list, so you can
          jump straight to the question you care about.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Come back tomorrow.</strong> A new topic each day, one story at a time, slowly builds a fuller picture
          of the world.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Above all, come with an open mind. Backstory won’t have the final answer to every question. It tries to show why
        things are complicated, set out the different points of view, and avoid easy explanations.
      </p>

      <h2>How issues are made</h2>
      <p>
        Backstory is researched and drafted with AI. For each issue, an AI research assistant (currently Claude, made by
        Anthropic) reads widely across news reports, official documents, data and reference sources, then writes the
        explainer to a fixed structure.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Every source is listed.</strong> Each issue ends with a numbered list of the reports and documents it draws
          on, with links, so you can check anything yourself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Contested claims are attributed.</strong> When governments, organisers and media give different numbers,
          or when a claim comes from one side of an argument, the issue says who is saying it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Being named is not proof.</strong> When a person or company is mentioned because an official body or
          report named them, that is reported as such, not as a finding of wrongdoing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>AI makes mistakes.</strong> Fast-moving stories change by the hour and AI research can get details wrong.
          Please check anything important against the original sources.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Photos</h2>
      <p>
        Backstory only uses freely licensed photos, mostly from Wikimedia Commons. The photographer, source and licence are
        credited under every image. Backstory never uses photos from news agencies or news sites.
      </p>

      <h2>Meet the creator</h2>
      <p>
        Hi, I’m {SITE.owner}! I studied Economics at the National University of Singapore, and I’m chronically tired but
        endlessly curious.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’ve always loved learning about the world. Back when I had more free time, I would lose whole afternoons to
        Wikipedia rabbit holes: historical events, economic crises, obscure geopolitical conflicts. One question led to
        another, and before I knew it the day was gone.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then adulthood happened, and I no longer have hours to research whatever catches my eye. But I still want to
        learn. I want to be better read and understand the issues that matter, while still having time for my other
        interests and, well, actually living my life.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I built Backstory: a way to make learning more manageable, for me and for anyone who shares the same
        curiosity. A place to explore complex issues, ask better questions and slowly become better informed, without
        doing hours of research first.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’m no expert on most of the topics covered here. I’m learning alongside you, and that’s exactly the point.
      </p>

      <p class="signoff">One story a day. A deeper understanding of the world.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
